  Sure! Here is an algorithm that takes two integers `a` and `b` as input and returns the smallest common multiple (LCM) of `a` and `b`:

1. Calculate the prime factors of both `a` and `b`:
	* Use the prime factorization of each number to find the prime factors that are common to both numbers.
	* These common prime factors will be the LCM of `a` and `b`.
2. Find the smallest number of the common prime factors that is greater than or equal to both `a` and `b`:
	* This is the smallest common multiple of `a` and `b`.

Here is some sample pseudocode for this algorithm:
```
function lcm(a, b) {
  // Calculate the prime factors of both a and b
  var factorsA = primeFactors(a);
  var factorsB = primeFactors(b);

  // Find the common prime factors
  var commonFactors = [];
  for (var i = 0; i < factorsA.length; i++) {
    if (factorsA[i] === factorsB[i]) {
      commonFactors.push(factorsA[i]);
    }
  }

  // Find the smallest number of the common prime factors that is greater than or equal to both a and b
  var lcm = commonFactors[0];
  for (var i = 1; i < commonFactors.length; i++) {
    lcm = Math.min(lcm * commonFactors[i], lcm);
  }

  return lcm;
}

function primeFactors(n) {
  // Use the Sieve of Eratosthenes to find the prime factors of n
  var factors = [];
  for (var i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    if (n % i === 0) {
      factors.push(i);
      while (n % i === 0) {
        n = n / i;
        factors.push(i);
      }
      break;
    }
  }
  return factors;
}
```
This algorithm has a time complexity of O(log n), where n is the number being factored, since it uses the Sieve of Eratosthenes to find the prime factors of `n`.

